s quad chip  being based on the m2 max dies instead  which will actually be a 12 core chip  and this is a big deal because the m2  max could easily have two die to die  interconnects like you see in this image  so apple would just have to rotate them  around to combine them into a quad die  chair and this actually makes the most  sense because it'll be much easier to  cool because you'll have such a large  package area to place the heatsink onto  instead of dealing with a dual stacked  die package but going even further  imagine boot on twitter leaked an  official apple schematic showing not one  but three ultra fusion interposer  connections and right there in the  corner is exactly what it looks like a  quad die chip with ultra fusion  connecting it vertically and  horizontally and making it even better  this source who leaked the schematic  says that he believes this quad chip  will be shipping within a new mac pro by  september which makes a wwdc reveal  extremely likely and the best part about  this leak is that it perfectly matches  up to ross young's leak for a new  27-inch mini led display that he  believes will be shipping by september  as well and he even told me that he  expects it to be revealed at wwdc  alongside the mac pro so i'm basically  100 sure about the mac pro coming at  wwdc but getting back to that leaked  diagram you'll notice that there are  multiple ultrafusion interposer chips  with varying sizes and before i can  explain how these are going to connect  all four of the dyes you absolutely must  understand how the current m1 ultra  chips ultra fusion works first so let's  get into that within that pdf from tsmc  there are some licensed images that i  unfortunately can't show you guys but  thankfully they actually made some of  these public so check this out and this  kaos s progress slide tsmc literally  showed off an image of the m1 ultras  interposer which matches up to apple's  marketing image of the m1 ultra chip you  have two large areas for the two m1 max  dies and eight smaller areas for the ram  chips so everything is basically placed  right onto that interposer now if you  don't know what an interposer is tsmc's  latest version can be up to 2500  millimeters squared in size and it  actually features five layers where  there can literally be direct metal  connections stitched through it and if  you have a hard time imagining what the  silicon interposer looks like imagine a  big block of jello consisting of five  different layers which can house a bunch  of metal connections and signals between  the m2 max dies and the unified memory  chips and to better explain this i dug  through apple's patents and i found this  patent image which is actually flipped  upside down and as you can see  104a and 104b are actually two m1 max  dies and this right here in the middle  is quite literally five different layers  of ultrafusion metal stitchings  connecting both of the dies together and  if you don't believe me when i say  stitchings here's another patent image  that i found showing the stitchings from  the side and the best part about the  silicon interposer is that it can  actually be used for routing memory  channels as you can see in this image of  the interposer sitting between the pcb  substrate which is the motherboard and  the multiple chips with five layers of  interposer visible in the center but  making things even better  there can also be tsv's or through  silicon vias which can allow connections  to be made vertically from the chips  directly into the pcb using the  redistribution layer which sends signals  horizontally and allows connections to  those tsv's which means that everything  like the io and the thunderbolt  connections can actually travel down  through the interposer and into the  motherboard instead of having to use a  traditional connection like we have on  the m1 max chip and then finally there  can also be an active interposer where  apple can embed dedicated chips and  controllers to manage power usage and  different various signals and whatever  else they want to do but the main point  you need to know is that apple starts  with a chip package and then places a  silicon interposer on top of it that  consists of five different layers and a  redistribution fan out layer which is  essentially what apple is using to make  those over 10 000 wired stitching  connections between both m1 max dies to  create the m1 ultra chip and now with  that said let's finally get into how  apple is gonna make the quad die m2  extreme chip work first of all tsmc says  that their new high performance silicon  interposer is limited to 2500  millimeters squared in size which is not  going to be enough for the massive quad  die m2 extreme including all of the ram  chips that they need on the package but  if you actually purchase the pdf you get  access to a big secret apparently they  have something called quadruple mask  stitching which literally allows them to  take four interposer squares and stitch  them together for a massive interposer  and in tsmc's testing there were no  issues with stability while doing this  and yes i confirmed with the source that  apple is in fact using quadruple mass  stitching for the quadi chip but now  getting into how apple is gonna stitch  the four dice together the most  interesting part is that apple's code  name for this new chip is red fern which  was apparently the first high-end  sportswear brand potentially known for  its sophisticated level of stitching and  speaking of that stitching a secret  source has confirmed that there will be  a total of 12 ultra fusion connections  between the four dies so that's three  connections per die which explains why  the leaked diagram has three ultra  fusions shown so i ended up discussing  all of this with ta tech15 on twitter  who shares my fascination for this type  of stuff and he was able to draw up a  bunch of diagrams showing how all this  is gonna work as you can see we have two  ultra chips on the left and the right  which already contain ultra fusion  connections between the top and bottom  m2 max dies which are labeled daisy 1  and daisy 2. but one thing you'll notice  is that both of the top dies have two  ultra fusion connections between them  both a daisy xl narrow and a daisy xl  wide which was shown in that league at  the top right corner and if you're  wondering why there's two of them it's  probably to make sure that there's  enough bandwidth since you're going from  one m2 ultra chip over to the other and  then finally there's the daisy xl sub 2  which doesn't look like it makes sense  until you look at this little drawing  showing that this connection wraps all  of them together in a lower level of the  interposer kind of like in an l shape  and you'll also notice a couple of dots  which are actually the controller chips  within the active interposer which sends  signals and controls the different dies  and if this doesn't seem like it's  possible the source confirmed that each  max die will be edited to have paths  integrated into the silicon to make all  of those connections possible so it's  definitely going to be more complicated  than it seems kind of like this diagram  with connections going all over the  place and because of the combination of  12 total connections between all the  dyes i have no doubt that apple will be  able to achieve essentially two times  scaling in terms of raw performance  going from the ultra to the ultra duo or  the extreme so if this new chip was  based on m1 max dies that would mean a  geekbench 5 multi-core score of around  48 000 points which would be competing  with some of the best amd epic server  chips out there like the 7713  which has an msrp of seven thousand  dollars alone and a tdp of 225 watts and  the craziest thing of all is that the  maximum tdp on the new m2 extreme should  be around 120 watts so half the power  usage for around the same performance  but wait those numbers are based on m1  max dies and we're almost certain at  this point that the new extreme chip  will be based on the m2 max which is  rumored to be a 12 chord ship instead of  10 like on the m1 max and this means  that the m2 extreme will be even more  powerful especially since we know that  the m2 will at least be based on the a15  bionic chips cores which as you can see  in this image are quite a bit larger  than the cores on the a14 chip which the  m1 max is based on and because of that  ta tech 15 draw a diagram showing that  the m2 max die should be quite a bit  larger as you can see with the dotted  line and this is very important because  it's going to give them a bit more room  to fit memory channels and i o around  the top and the left sides while packing  the dye to die interconnects on both the  bottom and the right side in orange and  this is going to be the secret sauce to  the m2 extreme because they'll be able  to rotate this new m2 max die around the  package and combine them all together  like we saw in that leaked apple  schematic and now with that said there  are two more topics to talk about  including how apple is gonna deal with  the ram and pci express slot situation  as well as how they're gonna cool down  this chip according to imagine boost  source the daisy xl connection that goes  into the sub two layer of the interposer  can apparently access a separate memory  controller to access up to  one terabyte of memory yes i said one  terabyte and he even told us that  currently the one terabyte of ram has  slower access times than on the older  xeon mac pro which basically confirms  that apple is going to have extra ram  slots on the motherboard that directly  connect to the m2 extreme chip using  that daisy xl sub 2 connection with a  built-in controller so what we're most  likely to see is the m2 extreme coming  with  gigs of unified memory built onto the  actual package and then a bunch of extra  ram slots just so that the real pros who  need that much ram can still pay for it  however i was reading through tsmc's pdf  document and it mentioned that this new  fifth generation of calos s technology  supports eight stacks of hbm3 memory and  the beautiful thing about using hbm is  that it'll allow them to save a ton of  package space while greatly increasing  bandwidth and allowing for up to one  terabyte of memory on the package itself  but i think this is going to be way too  expensive and just overkill in terms of  the bandwidth so i think that apple is  going to be sticking to traditional lp  ddr ram especially since they can use  the interposer layers to route the ram  connections through the substrate which  hishnash on twitter has confirmed to me  and then they can get creative with the  ram placement like in these images that  hishnash mocked up or like in this  example that i made a few months ago in  this m1 max quad video and now as for  the pci express slot situation while i  personally don't think that they're  going to want to keep supporting  third-party amd graphics cards i do  think there's a very good chance that  apple is working on their very own  external gpu cards and the reason for my  speculation on this is a little  something called lyfuca which was a  leaked code name for a dedicated gpu  meant for the imac pro and that makes  sense because lifuka is an island on the  kingdom of tonga which just so happens  to be another code name for the m1 chip  so i believe by the whole island  codename apple's engineers implied that  this apple silicon gpu was meant to be  separated from the main soc package and  i think we'll be seeing it in the new  mac pro and i think it's going to be  directly connected to the m2 extreme  using the daisy xl sub 2 connection and  now finally for the cooling part of the  video since i don't think they'll be  stacking m2 ultra chips anymore it's  going to be a lot easier to cool so the  first thing we need to do is figure out  how much power the m2 extreme w